We have the following events: <br><br>[latex]A[/latex] : student does well in maths <br>[latex]B[/latex] : student does well in physics <br>[latex]P(A^{c})[/latex] : student doesn't do well in maths <br>[latex]P(B^{c})[/latex] : student doesn't do well in physics <br> [latex]P(A^{c} \cap B^{c})[/latex] : student doesn't do well both maths and physics<br><br> Using DeMorgan's laws, we have: <br>[latex]
A^{c} \cap B^{c} = (A \cup B)^{c}
[/latex] <br><br> This gives us: <br>[latex]
\begin{aligned}
P(A^{c} \cap B^{c}) &= P(A \cup B)^{c}\\
&= 1 - P(A \cup B)\\
\end{aligned}
[/latex]<br><br> We can now compute [latex]P(A \cup B)[/latex] :<br> [latex]
\begin{aligned}
P(A \cup B) &= P(A) + P(B) - P(A \cap B)\\
&= 0.6 + 0.7 - 0.5\\
&= 0.8
\end{aligned}
[/latex] <br><br>The required probability is therefore [latex]{0.2}[/latex] .

If [latex]f[/latex] is odd, then [latex]f(-x) = -f(x)[/latex]. Then:<br> <br>[latex]
\begin{aligned}
g(-x) &= f(-x) - 2\\
&= -f(x) - 2\\
&= -[f(x) + 2]
\end{aligned}
[/latex]<br><br> This doesn't allow us to conclude anything about the nature of [latex]g[/latex]. A simple counter-example will show that if [latex]f[/latex] is odd, [latex]g[/latex] need not be odd. For instance, if [latex]f(x)= x[/latex], we have [latex]g(x) = x - 2[/latex], which is neither odd nor even. <br><br>If [latex]f[/latex] is even, then [latex]f(-x) = f(x)[/latex]. Then:<br><br> [latex]
\begin{aligned}
g(-x) &= f(-x) - 2\\
&= f(x) - 2\\
&= g(x)
\end{aligned}
[/latex] <br><br>Therefore, if [latex]f[/latex] is even, then [latex]g[/latex] is also even. A similar argument holds for the case when [latex]g[/latex] is even.

This limit has [latex]\cfrac{0}{0}[/latex] form. So we can try the L'Hopital's rule.<br><br> [latex]
\begin{aligned}
\lim \limits_{x \rightarrow 2} \cfrac{x^6 -24x -16}{x^3 +2x - 12} &= \lim \limits_{x \rightarrow 2} \cfrac{6x^5 -24}{3x^2 + 2} = 12
\end{aligned}
[/latex] <br><br>Note that the L'Hopital rule works here because the quotient of the derivatives of the numerator and denominator exists and is equal to [latex]12[/latex].

[latex]
\begin{aligned}
    \lim_{x \rightarrow \infty} \sqrt{x^2 + x + 1} - \sqrt{x^2 + 1} &= \lim_{x \rightarrow \infty} \cfrac{(x^2 + x + 1) - (x^2 + 1)}{\sqrt{x^2 + x + 1} + \sqrt{x^2 + 1}}\\\\
    &= \lim_{x \rightarrow \infty} \cfrac{x}{\sqrt{x^2 + x + 1} + \sqrt{x^2 + 1}}\\\\
