We have:<br> [latex]
f( x) =x^{3} -6x
[/latex] <br><br>The first and second derivatives are: <br>[latex]
f^{\prime }( x) =3x^{2} -6,\ f^{\prime \prime} ( x) =6x
[/latex]<br><br> The critical points of [latex] f[/latex] are: [latex]
\begin{aligned}
3x^{2} -6 & =0\\
x^{2} & =2\\
x & =\pm \sqrt{2}
\end{aligned}
[/latex] <br><br>We have two critical points. Now for the determination of the nature of the optimum: <br>[latex]
\begin{aligned}
f^{\prime \prime} \left(\sqrt{2}\right) &  >0\\
f^{\prime \prime} \left( -\sqrt{2}\right) & < 0
\end{aligned}
[/latex]<br> [latex] \sqrt{2}[/latex] is a local minimum and [latex] -\sqrt{2}[/latex] is a local maximum.

