\lim\limits _{x\rightarrow -3} \ \ \frac{\sqrt{2x+22} -4}{x+3}
[/latex]

raw_source.solution:
We can express the Taylor series expansion for [latex] \sqrt{2x+22}[/latex] around [latex] x=-3[/latex] as: <br><br>[latex]

4+\frac{1}{4}( x+3) +( x+3)^{2} p( x)

[/latex]<br><br> where [latex] p( x)[/latex] is some polynomial of [latex] x[/latex].<br><br> [latex]

\begin{aligned}
\lim\limits _{x\rightarrow -3} \ \ \frac{\sqrt{2x+22} -4}{x+3} & =\lim\limits _{x\rightarrow -3} \ \ \frac{\frac{1}{4}( x+3) +( x+3)^{2} p( x)}{x+3}\\
& \\
& =\frac{1}{4}
\end{aligned}

[/latex]

Let [latex] f( x) =\frac{\pi \sin x}{x}[/latex]. The slopes of the tangents to the curve at the two given points are [latex] f^{\prime }( \pi )[/latex] and [latex] f^{\prime }( -\pi )[/latex].<br><br> [latex]

\begin{aligned}
f^{\prime }( x) & =\pi \left[\frac{x\cos x-\sin x}{x^{2}}\right]
\end{aligned} \Longrightarrow \begin{aligned}
f^{\prime }( \pi ) & =-1\\
f^{\prime }( -\pi ) & =1
\end{aligned}

[/latex] <br><br>Since the slopes satisfy [latex] f^{\prime }( \pi ) \cdot f^{\prime}( -\pi ) =-1[/latex], the two tangents are perpendicular to each other. Hence the angle between them is [latex] {90^{\circ }}[/latex].

Let [latex] ( x_{0},x_{0})[/latex] be the point of tangency. Since the slope of the tangent is [latex] 1[/latex], we have [latex] 2x_{0} =1\Longrightarrow x_{0} =\frac{1}{2}[/latex]. Using the equation of the parabola, we get:<br><br> [latex]

\frac{1}{2} =\frac{1}{4} +C\Longrightarrow C=\frac{1}{4}

[/latex] <br><br>Since the curves are simple enough, let us visualize this: <br><br><img src="/wp-content/uploads/GATE/DA/MOCK/26031/a18.webp"/><br>

