raw_source.solution:
Matrix multiplication doesn't increase the rank. To see why note that we can view matrix multiplication in the following way. If [latex]B = \begin{bmatrix}b_1 & \cdots & b_n\end{bmatrix}[/latex] has [latex]n[/latex] columns, then [latex]AB[/latex] can be written as: <br><br>[latex]
AB = \begin{bmatrix}
Ab_1 & \cdots & Ab_n
\end{bmatrix}
[/latex]<br><br> We see that the [latex]i^{th}[/latex] column of [latex]AB[/latex] is a linear combination of the columns of [latex]A[/latex] , with the multipliers coming from the [latex]i^{th}[/latex] column of [latex]b[/latex]. Thus the column space of [latex]AB[/latex] is a subspace of the column space of [latex]A[/latex]. It follows that [latex]\text{rank}(AB) \leqslant \text{rank}(A)[/latex].<br><br> We could also view matrix multiplication in another way. If [latex]A = \begin{bmatrix}r_1^T\\\vdots\\r_m^T\end{bmatrix}[/latex] has [latex]m[/latex] rows, then [latex]AB[/latex] can be written as:<br><br> [latex]
AB = \begin{bmatrix}
r_1^TB\\
\vdots\\
r_m^TB
\end{bmatrix}
[/latex] <br><br>We see that the [latex]i^{th}[/latex] row of [latex]AB[/latex] is a linear combination of the rows of [latex]B[/latex] , with the multipliers coming form the [latex]i^{th}[/latex] row of [latex]A[/latex]. Thus the row space of [latex]AB[/latex] is a subspace of the row space of [latex]B[/latex]. It follows that [latex]\text{rank}(AB) \leqslant \text{rank}(B)[/latex].<br><br> Combining the two inequalities, we have: <br>[latex]
\text{rank}(AB) \leqslant \min(\text{rank}(A), \text{rank}(B))
[/latex] <br><br>Thus if we start with a matrix [latex]A[/latex] and multiply it by some matrix [latex]B[/latex] , the rank of the resulting matrix cannot be more than the rank of [latex]A[/latex].

[latex] P[/latex] is a projection transformation for [latex] \mathbb{R}^{n}[/latex] onto the vector [latex] v=( v_{1} ,\cdots ,v_{n})[/latex]. Then for any [latex] x\in \mathbb{R}^{n}[/latex] :<br><br> [latex]
\begin{aligned}
P( x) & =\frac{x^{T} v}{v^{T} v} v
\end{aligned}
[/latex] <br><br>The matrix corresponding to this transformation is given by: <br><br>[latex]
P=\begin{bmatrix}
| &  & |\\
P( e_{1}) & \cdots  & P( e_{n})\\
| &  & |
\end{bmatrix} =\frac{1}{v^{T} v}\begin{bmatrix}
| &  & |\\
v_{1} v & \cdots  & v_{n} v\\
| &  & |
\end{bmatrix} =\frac{1}{v^{T} v} vv^{T}
[/latex] <br><br>We have [latex] P_{ii} =\frac{v_{i}^{2}}{v^{T} v}[/latex].<br> Summing this from [latex] i=1[/latex] to [latex] i=n[/latex] , we get the trace as [latex] 1[/latex].

For a matrix to be the covariance matrix of some dataset, it has to be symmetric positive semi-definite. Consider a symmetric matrix:<br> [latex]

C=\begin{bmatrix}
d_{1} & a\\
a & d_{2}
\end{bmatrix}

[/latex]<br><br>For [latex] C[/latex] to be positive semi-definite, its eigenvalues have to be non-negative. The characteristic polynomial is: <br>[latex]

( d_{1} -\lambda )( d_{2} -\lambda ) -a^{2} =\lambda ^{2} -( d_{1} +d_{2}) \lambda +\left( d_{1} d_{2} -a^{2}\right)

[/latex] <br><br>For the eigenvalues to be non-negative, we see that: <br>[latex] d_{1} +d_{2} \geqslant 0[/latex] and [latex] d_{1} d_{2} -a^{2} \geqslant 0[/latex] <br><br>From this, we conclude that both [latex] d_{1}[/latex] and [latex] d_{2}[/latex] have to be non-negative, with [latex] |a|\leqslant \sqrt{d_{1} d_{2}}[/latex]. From the matrices given in the option, we see that (1) and (4) satisfy these conditions.

The total variance is the sum of the eigenvalues of the covariance matrix:<br> [latex]

\lambda _{1} +\cdots +\lambda _{5}

[/latex] <br><br>The first eigenvalue has to be [latex] 2[/latex]. Therefore, the percentage variance captured is: <br>[latex]

\frac{\lambda _{1}}{\lambda _{1} +\cdots +\lambda _{5}} =\frac{2}{\lambda _{1} +\cdots +\lambda _{5}}

[/latex]<br><br> To get the least variance, the denominator has to be the largest. This happens if all eigenvalues are non-zero: <br>[latex]

\frac{2}{2+2+2+2+2} =\frac{1}{5}

[/latex] <br><br>Expressed as a percentage, we get [latex] 20\%[/latex].

Since the eigenvectors of [latex] \mathbf{C}[/latex] are themselves the standard basis vectors of [latex] \mathbb{R}^{3}[/latex], [latex] \mathbf{C}[/latex] is diagonal with the eigenvalues occupying the diagonal entries:<br> <br>[latex]

\mathbf{C} =\begin{bmatrix}
\lambda _{1} &  & \\
& \lambda _{2} & \\
&  & \lambda _{3}
\end{bmatrix}

[/latex] <br><br>For a valid covariance matrix, the eigenvalues are non-negative. Recall that the eigenvalues represent the variance along the principal components. A sample dataset that has this kind of a structure:<br><br> [latex]

\mathbf{X} =\begin{bmatrix}
1 & 0 & -1 & 0\\
0 & 1 & 0 & -1\\
0 & 0 & 0 & 0
\end{bmatrix} \Longrightarrow \mathbf{C} =\begin{bmatrix}
0.5 &  & \\
& 0.5 & \\
&  & 0
\end{bmatrix}

[/latex] <br><br>Visually, these are four points that lie on the XY plane, hence there is no variance along the third dimension.

Each data-point can belong to one of the [latex]k[/latex] clusters. Therefore, we have: <br>[latex]
k \times \cdots \times k = k^{n}
[/latex]

